"""Authored cast voices, ordered Silas/Kade/Ashe/Nico/Riven/Emi/Vera/Lume/Mochi/Zuri.

Subject reuse is deliberate and canonicalized per character: a line is never
repeated merely because another map contains the same subject.
"""
VOICE={}
def row(key,emotion,lines):
 values=lines.split('|');assert len(values)==10,(key,len(values))
 VOICE[key]=[(emotion,v.strip()) for v in values]

row('road','soft_smile',"I packed three notebooks and forgot a spoon. An instructive distribution of priorities.|Easy pace. We have somewhere to go, not something to prove to the road.|Our footsteps keep drifting into the same rhythm. That is a nice accidental duet.|We should stop before hungry becomes our principal personality.|The road is my longest-running stage. Difficult acoustics. Excellent exits.|A road is a very long sentence. These shoes object to the punctuation.|A decent path makes room for different speeds. I like whoever built this one.|Someone walked here before it was a path. I wonder what they were hoping to find.|Your sleeves are catching the wind. Travelling clothes finally getting to travel.|There are about six greens in that verge. I only packed three pencils. Personal crisis.")
row('grass','neutral',"Something moved before the grass did. Small observations are how fieldwork starts.|Watch the verge. Tiny things deserve warning before the boot arrives.|Listen between the rustles. There is a whole percussion section in there.|The grass is full of residents. We are the unexpected doorbell.|An excellent hiding place, provided the audience is shorter than the grass.|The grass has an audience. I hope my walking reviews are kind.|Those little tracks are useful. We can share a path without flattening someone's front door.|My grandmother called this grass whispering. I think she was reminding us to listen.|A loose thread catches on everything out here. That is a design problem, not your fault.|Look at that little trail. Somebody very small has a very definite destination.")
row('rest','soft_smile',"Rest is part of the method. I am writing that down so I cannot argue later.|Sit a minute. Being tired is information, not a character flaw.|We do not have to fill every quiet moment. This one can just be quiet.|If you need a break, I have professional qualifications in sitting near snacks.|An intermission! Nobody is required to make it dramatically meaningful.|We can stop. The horizon has not filed a deadline.|An overheating engine needs a pause. So do people, and we are much harder to replace.|There is no story requirement to keep walking when your feet hurt.|Comfort is a legitimate feature. You are allowed to use it.|We can look at something without trying to turn it into progress.")
row('sea','neutral',"The tide changes everything at the edge without asking for a new map. Fascinating.|Check the footing. The sea has no interest in our confidence.|The waves keep changing the rhythm without losing the song.|Salty air makes me hungry. In my defence, so does ordinary air.|Behold, an ocean! I shall attempt the modest trick of keeping my boots dry.|The sea is very committed to the dramatic entrance. Every few seconds.|Salt gets into everything. Remind me to clean the hinges before they become modern sculpture.|People leave things at the shore for reasons. I try not to invent the reasons for them.|A good coat needs pockets that close. The sea is an enthusiastic collector.|I always paint water too blue. Look properly and it is borrowing colours from everything.")
row('forest','soft_smile',"The roots leave room for each other more often than I expected. I should study that.|Leave the branches alone. We can get through without taking the place apart.|The canopy makes every sound feel closer. Even my very dignified stomach.|I know three edible mushrooms. Today I am choosing the safety of a sandwich.|The trees have arranged their own curtain. I feel underdressed for the production.|A forest makes an excellent library. Terrible signage, though.|Roots solved the support problem without a single screw. Mildly threatening to my profession.|This feels like the sort of place a story asks you to be polite. Good advice anywhere.|Everything catches on a cape here. Nature has strong opinions about silhouettes.|The light moves across the trunks like someone is changing their mind about the picture.")
row('cave','neutral',"Please do not shout for an echo. We are not the only things listening.|Mind your head. The cave will not be submitting an apology.|An echo gives back every mistake. Harsh rehearsal space.|If something smells like soup in here, we are not eating it.|Excellent atmosphere. Poor evacuation lighting. I have notes.|I was going to say something haunting. The ceiling already has the assignment.|A torch is useful. Knowing where the exit is remains more useful.|People hear voices in echoes. Sometimes it is better to ask who else is in the cave.|Good grip, short steps. Looking elegant is currently an optional objective.|I like those mineral colours. I like leaving the wall intact even more.")
row('heat','annoyed',"My notes say to drink water before becoming a cautionary example.|Water first. Toughness can wait in the shade.|I have composed a short piece entitled Please Find Some Shade.|I could toast bread on this rock. We should probably move off it.|My dramatic cape has become a regrettable portable oven.|The sun has mistaken me for an unfinished roast.|Heat changes tolerances. Including mine. Let us check on everyone.|The old stories say to respect the mountain. Today that mostly means bringing water.|Dark fabric was a decision. I am accepting feedback from the weather.|Look at the colours if you like, but keep drinking. Heatstroke is not artistic commitment.")
row('rain','soft_smile',"The waterproof notebook has proved its worth. My allegedly waterproof socks have resigned.|Wet straps chafe. Say something before it becomes a bigger problem.|Rain on leaves, rain on a roof, rain on us. Three different drum kits.|This weather makes hot bread feel like a public service.|Rain reveals exactly how little a grand gesture helps an umbrella.|The sky is making my hair more expressive than my face.|I sealed the tool roll. I have finally outsmarted one puddle.|A rainy road is still a road. We can take it slowly.|I brought a spare dry layer. Being prepared should feel kind, not smug.|The rain changes every surface. I could sketch for hours. From somewhere with a roof.")
row('sand','neutral',"Those tiny footprints suggest a very busy night. I hope everyone found breakfast.|Sand in your shoes? Stop now. It does not become more comfortable through loyalty.|The wind is playing everything except a tune I can remember.|Sand is not an acceptable seasoning. I am writing that on the lunch bag.|The landscape has erased my footprints. At last, a competent assistant.|The sand keeps a diary and the wind edits it. Poor archival standards.|Sand will find the one unsealed joint. It has an engineering degree in persistence.|A place can look empty and still be full of lives. Watch where you step.|These boots were designed for a road. We are negotiating new terms.|Every ridge has its own shadow. A quiet place is not an uninteresting place.")
row('home','soft_smile',"A home is more than a useful address. I am still learning how to make time for mine.|You can want to go somewhere and still miss home. Both fit in one bag.|Hearing a familiar door open is a sound I could never quite put into a song.|The best thing about visiting home is somebody insisting you need a second helping.|A homecoming does not require an impressive entrance. I remind myself frequently.|You do not have to earn the right to come back tired.|A repair is easier when someone kept the little missing piece. People do that too.|Some houses are full of stories nobody has written down. Ask while someone can answer.|A worn sleeve can mean somebody has had a good long life in a favourite coat.|Home is allowed to change without becoming somewhere you never belonged.")
row('book','neutral',"Someone revised this book before printing it. I find that detail extremely comforting.|Check the page before folding it. Someone else may need that map.|I like marginal notes. A very small duet between people who never met.|There had better be a recipe index. I have been betrayed by decorative chapter titles.|An unreliable narrator? Finally, professional representation.|Books let people finish a sentence centuries after somebody interrupted them.|Good diagrams. A manual that assumes I have only two hands deserves a medal.|The oldest version is not automatically the truest. I would like to know who was allowed to write it.|The binding has been repaired carefully. Somebody wanted this one to keep being read.|Look at the ink marks. Even something printed perfectly has been through someone's hands.")
row('picturebook','laughing',"A diagram is not cheating. Some ideas are considerate enough to have pictures.|Clear instructions. A rare and valuable literary genre.|The drawings have better stage presence than I do.|An illustrated cake. Cruel, but beautifully executed.|I approve of a book that lets the scenery take a bow.|A picture book. Finally, a narrator who knows when to stop talking.|Exploded diagrams! Ideally the only kind of explosion involved.|Someone chose what to leave out of this picture. That can be a story too.|The illustrations understand pockets. We have a serious author here.|A little drawing can make someone feel welcome before they read a word.")
row('vase','neutral',"The empty space is part of the design. I am defending this on behalf of my desk.|Looks fragile. We can admire it without stress-testing the owner.|Tap it and it might ring. Please do not. I am learning restraint.|A lovely container that currently refuses to be a mixing bowl. Fair enough.|Nothing inside. A devastating review of my last disappearing trick.|An empty vase. Quite confident about its potential.|A narrow base and a heavy top. I have met people with more stable plans.|Someone may be waiting for flowers. Or they may just like an empty vase.|The shape is doing all the work. No need to glue a bow to it. This time.|The shadow through that handle is almost as interesting as the vase.")
row('trash','neutral',"No sample collection without permission. That includes scientific curiosity near a bin.|Nothing we need. Let it keep its privacy.|An empty bin. The audience for my discarded song titles has gone home.|We are not doing ingredients by surprise today.|An empty container! I am inexplicably being outperformed again.|Nothing in the bin. A refreshingly restrained plot twist.|Before you salvage a part, find out why it was thrown away.|Some things are allowed to be finished. We do not need to rescue every scrap.|Repair what you can. Keeping every broken thing is a different hobby.|A clean bin tells me someone has been doing work that usually goes unnoticed.")
row('shop','neutral',"I have enough equipment to research restraint. A difficult subject.|Check what we actually need before the display starts making decisions.|There is music here specifically designed to make shopping feel like a good idea.|We can buy supplies or the whole window display. I have arguments for both.|The prices are performing the most convincing vanishing act in town.|I have mistaken wanting a thing for needing a thing. My bag has evidence.|A useful tool should solve a problem you already have.|A charm can mean something without costing three meals.|We can admire a design without buying it. I would like this printed on my wallet.|Someone chose these colours to make us pause. It is working; I resent their competence.")
row('blueprint','soft_smile',"A plan shows what someone hopes will work. The corrections are often the interesting part.|Those arrows need labels. An arrow cannot carry the parcel by itself.|I understand the little rehearsal marks. Apparently buildings need them too.|I can identify the kitchen. I consider this a strong opening.|A stage before the stage. How delightfully presumptuous.|A building drawn before it exists. I do this with conversations. Less successfully.|They left space to service the mechanism. That is somebody thinking about the next person.|Plans and stories both get people somewhere. Both benefit from admitting where the gaps are.|How will someone move through it carrying groceries? Important design question.|The corrections make this drawing feel alive. An idea has been allowed to change.")
row('computer','neutral',"Save a second copy. My faith in machinery has very specific limits.|Useful screen. Also a convenient reminder to stand up occasionally.|I name my music files carefully now. Final Final Really Final was a difficult era.|A computer cannot tell whether a recipe is good. I have tested this on several computers.|It can remember every number except the password I need. A kindred performer.|Someone's work is in there. Curiosity is not a password.|Please do not press the button labelled RESET to see what happens.|A record is not the whole person. Remember that when a screen makes things look simple.|The cables have been tied neatly. Somebody cared about what people would never see.|A blank screen reflects us. Slightly judgmental for a machine with no eyebrows.")
row('machine','surprised',"I would like to read the instructions before admiring the alarming part.|It is humming. I would prefer to learn why from over here.|That rhythm is uneven. Machinery should not be attempting jazz without supervision.|Any machine that large should make something better than toast. Unless it makes excellent toast.|A magnificent device. I shall resist touching its mysterious dramatic lever.|The machine is making an ominous noise. Very committed to the genre.|Those moving parts need a guard. A warning sign is not a guard.|If someone calls it a miracle, I still want to know where the power comes from.|There is a difference between complicated and useful. This one is auditioning for both.|Someone built that from a drawing. I like remembering that before it feels inevitable.")
row('food','soft_smile',"A meal is a perfectly respectable reason to pause an experiment.|That belongs to someone. We can ask rather than develop a theory of unattended lunch.|A kitchen sounds like an orchestra that expects dinner to happen afterwards.|Whoever made this cared about the edges. You can tell. Also I am hungry.|I shall perform my most difficult trick: leaving someone else's dessert alone.|The food smells good. A devastatingly original observation. I stand by it.|A good kitchen is a workshop where the mistakes are occasionally delicious.|Food can carry a family story without needing anyone to explain it out loud.|There is room at that table for one more chair. I like places that notice.|Look at how the dishes have been set out. Somebody wanted the meal to feel welcoming.")
row('privacy','neutral',"Observation is not permission. We can leave their things alone.|Not ours. That is the entire investigation.|We do not need to open somebody's drawer to have an interesting afternoon.|Even an exceptionally promising biscuit tin can still be somebody else's tin.|There are secrets I only open with an invitation. This is one of them.|A closed drawer is an excellent place to keep a boundary.|If the owner wants a repair, they can ask us. Otherwise the lid stays down.|Wondering about a person does not give us a right to their keepsakes.|A wardrobe is somebody's private collection of selves. Let it stay private.|Their things, their choice. We have plenty of our own pockets to lose stuff in.")
row('burglary','annoyed',"Someone has already treated this place like its people did not matter. We can do better.|Nothing to take. Someone to check on.|I do not want to make a joke about this. They deserve a little quiet.|We can ask what would help. A proper meal, maybe. Not an audience.|Taking something is not a trick just because you were clever about it.|An empty place can still belong to someone. Let us remember that.|Start by asking what they need. Fixing things without asking can make a second mess.|They are allowed to be upset about objects. Sometimes an object holds a person in your memory.|If they want help putting things back, I can mend what is torn.|Whoever did this wanted to make the room feel small. We can stay and make it less lonely.")
row('art','soft_smile',"I like how it changes when you stand somewhere else. A useful principle beyond paintings.|Someone spent time on that. We do not have to understand it to respect the work.|It makes me think of a song without words. Conveniently, I know several unfinished ones.|The colours remind me of fruit. I am prepared to admit that may be a personal problem.|The frame has excellent stage presence. The picture seems quite secure about it.|I like it. I am trying to let that be enough before inventing a clever opinion.|I keep thinking about how they made it. Then I remember to look at what they made.|The artist is not here to explain it. We can be curious without pretending to know their whole life.|The colours do not match in the obvious way. They still make room for each other.|You can see where they changed their mind. Please tell me nobody is going to paint over that bit.")
row('phone','neutral',"Calling home counts as using the equipment responsibly.|If you have been putting off a call, I can wait outside.|I rehearse phone calls. Then they say something I did not put in the rehearsal.|We could call ahead and ask where the bakery is. A strategic use of technology.|A voice appearing from a box! The competition has become unreasonable.|The telephone permits long-distance awkward silences. Ambitious invention.|A little wire makes someone far away feel close. That is good engineering.|It is strange how one familiar voice can change the whole room.|If we say we will call, let us write it down and actually do it.|You do not need a good reason to tell someone you thought of them.")
row('tools','soft_smile',"A place for each tool. My desk has adopted a more theoretical approach.|Put it back where it came from. The next person may need it in a hurry.|The tools are arranged like notes. I would like to avoid playing the sharp ones.|That is a better-organised workbench than my last attempt at breakfast.|Every prop has a place. Otherwise the rabbit appears holding a wrench.|Organised tools. A startling piece of speculative fiction for my bag.|Those handles are worn smooth. Someone has spent years learning with these.|A well-kept tool can be a kind of family story.|The small repair kit is the important accessory. It just gets very little applause.|The paint on that handle has worn away where a thumb rests. I love that detail.")
row('nurse','soft_smile',"She noticed the tired Pokemon before we asked. That is a skill worth studying.|We will say thank you and actually let the team rest.|A calm voice changes the room. She is very good at it.|People who look after everyone deserve somebody bringing them lunch.|No flourish, no applause, just very good work. I respect it.|She makes care look ordinary. That does not make it small.|Good equipment helps. Somebody knowing when you are worried matters too.|A place like this becomes part of many people's stories. Usually at the part where they needed help.|I hope her shoes are comfortable. Looking after people involves a lot of standing.|You can tell when a room has been designed around care instead of appearances.")
row('scientist','neutral',"A good question can survive being asked by somebody without a lab coat.|I like an explanation that leaves room for questions.|They speak about their work the way I wish I spoke about music.|A scientist who remembers lunch is someone I can work with.|An excellent presentation. I would request fewer statements of absolute certainty.|I admire the confidence. I am reserving judgment on the ominous machine.|Ask what happens when it fails. That is a compliment to the design, honestly.|Recording a thing and understanding it are two different chapters.|The coat has useful pockets. An encouraging research priority.|I like seeing someone light up when they get to explain what they love.")
row('hiker','neutral',"That pack has more volume than its owner. I would like the weight distribution notes.|Good boots. Better advice about the weather.|You can hear the mountain in their voice. Mainly the amount of climbing involved.|They packed lunch for a mountain. Finally, somebody who understands the assignment.|A magnificent entrance, though the mountain appears to have followed them in on their boots.|I respect the commitment to going up something that was perfectly visible from below.|Check the straps, not just the size of the pack. The practical details keep you going.|People tell different stories about a mountain after they have had to walk back down it.|That is a well-mended pack. Fashion with a long and convincing employment history.|They know the view you only get by taking your time. I want to hear about that one.")
row('fisher','neutral',"They are observing patiently without calling it research. I may need to revise my definition.|A useful skill: knowing when pulling harder will only break the line.|Fishing has long rests and unexpected crescendos. I can follow that score.|Ask before assuming the catch is lunch. Some of them have names.|The suspense is excellent. I have concerns about the seating.|An entire afternoon dedicated to waiting politely. I would like that qualification.|There is a lot of engineering in a little reel. And considerable wisdom in not dropping it.|A person can sit quietly and still be doing something that matters to them.|Look at the reinforced elbows. Somebody dressed for the actual day they wanted.|They see a changing picture every minute and do not need to keep any of it.")
row('sailor','neutral',"You can hear how much weather they have learned to respect.|Someone who checks the knots twice is welcome on my team.|The sea shanties probably have practical verses I have been singing incorrectly.|A person who can cook on a moving ship has my immediate professional respect.|I enjoy their stories. I shall be keeping the ones that include safety instructions.|Their boat has survived more plot twists than most novels.|Salt, strain, weather. There is no room for pretending a repair is good enough.|A home can be a person you return to, even when your address keeps moving.|Good layers, no loose things near ropes. Practical outfits are full of good decisions.|I want to paint the harbour after hearing them describe coming home.")
row('gym','neutral',"Training carefully is different from simply making the team do more work.|Watch how the trainer responds when a plan fails. That tells you more than a speech.|A battle has a rhythm. Knowing when to change it matters.|Four different partners, one shared job. I have worked in kitchens with worse teamwork.|An audience helps. It should not become the reason a Pokemon has to keep performing.|The gym promises character development. My knees have requested a less ambitious syllabus.|A good test teaches you something useful even when the answer is no.|Strength is not the only thing worth learning from a battle.|A team can have a theme without making every member identical.|Look at how much room each Pokemon has to move. That is where the real design is.")
row('progress','soft_smile',"We learned something. I would like to celebrate before assigning the next experiment.|Good work. Now check on the team before making the next plan.|You did it. I had a congratulatory sentence ready and have forgotten every word.|That calls for something baked. This is my response to most meaningful events.|A thoroughly earned victory. I shall resist adding a confetti malfunction.|You did well. I was going to disguise that as a joke, but it can just be true.|That was adaptability, not luck. You can take credit for learning.|It is worth remembering this moment before the next task makes it feel small.|You made that work in your own way. Keep that bit.|Look at your team. They know they did something together.")
row('later_progress','soft_smile',"I am beginning to trust the part of a plan that says we will learn on the way.|You are getting better at asking for help before the trouble gets loud.|Your confidence sounds different now. Less like you are trying to convince yourself.|We have travelled far enough that the emergency snacks now have a history.|You have become inconveniently difficult to impress with a simple flourish.|I remember when leaving town felt enormous. It still counts that it did.|You change a plan without treating the old plan as a moral failure. Useful skill.|There are more people in our story now. I hope we have been kind enough in theirs.|Your old travelling clothes have earned their repairs.|We have seen enough places that I have stopped trying to fit everything onto one page.")
row('champion','soft_smile',"You earned this. I am putting the notebook away for a moment so I can actually be here.|Champion. Same boots, longer road behind them. I am glad I was on some of it.|I thought I would have a song ready. Right now I am just very happy for you.|I owe your team a feast. We may need a larger definition of oven.|A standing ovation seems appropriate. I am prepared to keep standing for some time.|You did it. There is no clever line that would improve that.|I could explain how hard that was. I would rather tell you how proud I am.|I will remember the small things as well as this ending. They got us here.|A title is lovely. The way you treated your team on the way here is lovelier.|I want to draw this exactly as it feels. That might take more than one picture.")
row('bike','laughing',"The experiment has acquired wheels. Please warn the notebooks before accelerating.|Check the brakes. Looking fast and stopping well are different skills.|A bicycle bell is a one-note instrument with remarkable social authority.|We are travelling faster than the snacks can settle. Bold logistical choice.|A wheeled entrance! Less dignified than a cape, considerably more efficient.|My legs have outsourced the journey to a circle. Excellent management.|Listen to that chain. It wants oil more than it wants encouragement.|We can go quickly without pretending the scenery stopped existing.|Tuck the loose fabric in. I refuse to lose a friend to an avoidable trouser incident.|The view is becoming a very long smear. I will sketch when we stop.")
row('underwater','surprised',"Every movement disturbs a whole small world. Let us be careful where we settle.|Stay where we can see each other. Interesting things are not worth getting separated over.|It is quiet in a way I did not know quiet could be.|My sandwich is above the water. For once I made an excellent advance decision.|I have rehearsed holding my breath. I have not rehearsed being a fish.|The sea has a downstairs. Disturbingly well furnished.|Water pressure has very little interest in enthusiasm. Keep the equipment checked.|Whole stories happened above this place. The sea does not owe us their secrets.|Everything moves differently down here. Even a loose ribbon can be a problem.|Those colours only exist because the light travelled all this way. I wish I could bring the feeling back.")
row('grave','sad',"We can leave the notebook closed. Not every moment needs to become a record.|We can stay a little. There is no delivery deadline here.|I do not have a song for this. I can still stand with you.|If someone needs company afterwards, I can make tea and listen.|No performance needed here. I will keep my voice down.|We do not have to find the right words before we are allowed to care.|I can fix a broken object. I am learning not to offer that as an answer to every kind of loss.|Remembering someone kindly does not have to look the same every day.|We can straighten a fallen flower if it is welcome. Small care still counts.|Someone keeps coming back to tend this place. That is part of the picture too.")
row('rocket','annoyed',"They call it research while ignoring whether anyone agreed. That is not a technical distinction.|A uniform does not make taking somebody's Pokemon acceptable.|I dislike the way they talk as though nobody else in the room can hear them.|Taking someone's partner is not a shortcut. It is choosing to be cruel.|Misdirection is a performance. Theft is theft. They do not get my vocabulary.|They keep confusing being feared with being interesting.|Power without anyone allowed to question it is a badly designed machine.|I am more interested in who they hurt than in the legend they tell about themselves.|Their team deserves better than being treated as an accessory to somebody's ego.|They want everyone else to be background. Let us make that difficult.")
row('legend','surprised',"I would like to observe before deciding what this encounter means.|Give it space. Impressive does not mean it belongs to us.|That is the sort of sound a song tries to describe and usually fails.|I have lost my appetite for approximately three seconds. Historic occasion.|I had prepared a grand introduction. It appears entirely unnecessary.|Some things make even my internal narrator stop for a moment.|Look at it. Then remember that knowing a name does not mean knowing what it needs.|I have heard stories about this. I would like to meet the living Pokemon, not just the story.|It does not need our approval to be extraordinary.|No picture is going to hold all of this. I am glad we are here anyway.")
row('wild_offer','soft_smile',"It seems to want a choice, not a conclusion written on its behalf.|We can make the offer. It gets to decide what that means.|That is a surprisingly familiar sort of nervous introduction.|I support any negotiation in which the lunch arrangements are made clear.|A willing assistant is a much better partner than a captive audience.|It has considered its options more thoroughly than I considered these shoes.|Start with what it needs. A good arrangement works for both sides.|An unusual meeting does not have to become an obligation. We can listen.|We do not have to change what makes it odd to make room for it.|Look at how it waits for an answer. Let us give it a proper one.")

# Individual settlements. These are observations about the actual place, not
# the original Pip line with another name pasted onto it.
row('LITTLEROOT_TOWN','soft_smile',"I recognise the sound of the laboratory door. It used to mean somebody might answer my questions.|Small town. People still notice whether you made it home.|It is quiet enough to hear somebody practising with the window open. Brave choice.|A place this small can identify a cake thief by the crumbs on their shoes.|A modest beginning. My favourite sort to exaggerate responsibly later.|The town looks smaller once you have left it. The leaving was still big.|Birch runs toward his research. I admire it while recommending better boots.|Every family here probably has a different story about the same tree.|Somebody is always airing a blanket here. That makes a place feel lived in.|The lab, the houses, the little paths. Somebody could fit a whole life into this view.")
row('OLDALE_TOWN','soft_smile',"I used to measure progress by how far I got from OLDALE. That was a rather unkind unit.|A crossroads and somewhere to rest. Sensible priorities for a town.|I like the way directions become little conversations here.|A shop and a Pokemon Center. Now for my entirely unbiased proposal: a bakery.|I could call this a crossroads of destiny. The sign has sensibly opted for directions.|A small town is allowed to matter without acquiring a dramatic secret.|The paths meet neatly. Somebody designed this without needing a grand entrance.|Places where people exchange directions collect more stories than they realise.|I would put a sturdy bench near that crossroads. Good design sometimes starts with somewhere to sit.|Every traveller changes this town for about five minutes. That is an interesting kind of home.")
row('PETALBURG_CITY','neutral',"Wally needed somebody to believe the first attempt was worth making. I understand that.|Your dad has a job here. You are still allowed to have feelings about visiting.|Family makes an ordinary doorway feel like a stage sometimes.|We can take a lap before the gym. I will claim it was a serious snack reconnaissance.|A family reunion and a battle venue. Very efficient use of dramatic tension.|You do not have to turn every family conversation into proof you are doing well.|Norman built his gym around a clear idea. I like a plan that leaves space for learning.|Wally's first journey is not smaller because somebody helped him begin.|A comfortable town can still contain a difficult conversation.|The gym draws the eye, but I want to sketch the little daily routes around it.")
row('RUSTBORO_CITY','neutral',"A school and a laboratory in one city. My childhood would have required a larger question budget.|The city has more straight lines than my last delivery plan. I am jealous.|The school bell still makes me feel late for something I have already finished.|A city full of stone buildings needs someone selling something warm.|A museum city has a ready audience for a good old story. I should bring accurate dates.|The buildings look certain of themselves. I wonder if the architects were.|DEVON is impressive. So is the person who knows where every spare part went.|A fossil is evidence and a story. It is worth being careful about which is which.|The uniforms at the school have survived some serious running. Good fabric choices.|Look at how the pale stone changes when a cloud passes. It is never just grey.")
row('DEWFORD_TOWN','soft_smile',"A town changes its favourite phrase by committee. An interesting social experiment.|The island is small. People still find plenty of ways to get lost in a conversation.|A tiny hall with a very loud opinion. I could play here and pretend the noise was applause.|Everyone knows the boat schedule. I want to know the bread schedule.|I could start a fashionable saying. For public safety, I shall refrain.|The whole town has a trend. I admire the speed of its editorial process.|The boats need repairs all year. Someone here has very patient hands.|An island story travels by boat and returns with new details.|Salt is unkind to clothes. Somebody here knows excellent mending tricks.|I could draw the harbour every day and never catch the same light twice.")
row('SLATEPORT_CITY','laughing',"There is a museum and a market. Education and the opportunity to lose all my money.|A harbour this busy runs on people remembering small promises.|The market sounds like six songs being rehearsed in the same room.|Finally. A city that understands lunch can have several departments.|A market is a theatre where every stall has decided to be the lead.|I came for the sea and stayed because the market has strong narrative momentum.|Hear that workshop? Somebody is making a ship useful again. Excellent music.|Sailors bring stories from places the market labels never quite explain.|The market has good sturdy bags. I am resisting the ones that merely look adventurous.|Bright awnings, weathered boats, people going places. I need an unreasonable amount of paper.")
row('MAUVILLE_CITY','neutral',"The lights are impressive. I wonder who gets called when one refuses to work.|A busy crossroads can make standing still feel like a rule violation. It is not.|Every machine here has decided it deserves a solo.|We should set a spending limit before anything starts flashing at us.|Mauville understands showmanship. My wallet has requested a quieter venue.|There are enough lights here to give midnight an identity crisis.|Wattson has made electricity a local personality. I respect the commitment.|A city can be very new and still collect stories about who built it.|Comfortable shoes matter here. Every interesting thing seems to be one more street away.|The lights make everyone's colours change. I hope nobody thinks their reflection is telling the whole truth.")
row('VERDANTURF_TOWN','soft_smile',"The air is supposed to be good for people. I like research that begins with somewhere pleasant to sit.|A quiet town is a useful place to admit you are worn out.|This is the sort of quiet that makes me want to write something gentle.|Someone here must make bread slowly. The whole town has the right pace.|Not every stop needs a spectacle. I am attempting to remember that professionally.|The town has given my thoughts a little more room. Slightly rude of it to be helpful.|A place designed around feeling better is a good place to learn from.|People come here to breathe more easily. I try not to fill that space with questions.|There is nothing wrong with choosing a place because it feels comfortable.|The colours are soft here without being dull. That is harder to paint than it sounds.")
row('FALLARBOR_TOWN','neutral',"Volcanic ash changes the soil. It also changes whether I can find my original sock colour.|Ash gets into straps. Let us check the team before heading farther.|The ash makes every footstep sound like the world has turned the volume down.|A farming town knows that good food begins well before a kitchen.|A dramatic sky. I would request a less aggressive special-effects department.|The ash makes everything look like it has been waiting for a confession.|A filter needs cleaning here more often than pride would prefer.|The fields have been worked through many kinds of weather. That is a long story.|The local clothes have practical layers. Style has clearly met the actual forecast.|There are warm colours under all that grey. You have to spend a moment looking.")
row('LAVARIDGE_TOWN','soft_smile',"I understand the geological explanation. I am still going to enjoy the hot water.|A town with built-in rest stops. Excellent infrastructure.|The steam takes the edge off every sound. Including the one my knees just made.|Warm water, warm food. We are dangerously close to my entire philosophy.|The steam provides a magnificent entrance. It also makes finding my towel difficult.|The town is built around being less tense. An unsettlingly persuasive concept.|Geothermal heating. Occasionally nature produces a very tidy design.|People have told stories around these springs for years. Usually after finding their towel.|The important outfit decision is whether you brought something dry afterwards.|The steam makes everyone look briefly like a rumour. I like it here.")
row('FORTREE_CITY','surprised',"They built around the trees rather than removing the problem. I admire that sort of answer.|Watch the bridges. I am not delivering either of us by gravity.|A whole town that moves a little with the wind. Excellent, terrifying acoustics.|Tree houses! I would like written confirmation that someone delivers groceries.|A city with entrances on several levels. My cape has been promoted to a safety concern.|The town has put its roots in the wrong direction. Somehow I trust it.|Those supports work with the living tree. That takes patience, not just a clever drawing.|The trees are older than the houses. I hope the oldest stories remember that.|Flexible walkways and practical clothes. A convincing argument against a trailing hem.|I do not know whether to draw the houses or the spaces between them. Both feel important.")
row('LILYCOVE_CITY','neutral',"A museum beside a department store. Two very different ways to collect things.|We can look at the display without signing our entire purse over to it.|The contest hall makes my stomach remember auditions. I can admire the sign from here.|That shop has enough floors to count as a hiking plan. Bring snacks.|An audience, a gallery, and shopping. A city with a worrying understanding of my weaknesses.|The department store has several levels of temptation. My boots are making excuses already.|I want to know how they move supplies through a building that tall.|A painting in the museum was somebody's ordinary afternoon once.|One can appreciate a contest outfit without believing every day needs one.|I want to hear what the museum staff love about the pictures when nobody is grading the answer.")
row('MOSSDEEP_CITY','surprised',"A space centre. I have briefly run out of sensible questions and am left with very large ones.|A rocket needs thousands of small things to work. Respect the person checking the last bolt.|The idea of a song reaching space is lovely. I should finish one that can reach the next room.|Do they test food for space? I am suddenly interested in a very specific career change.|At last, a launch with more dramatic ambition than mine.|The sky was apparently not far enough away for these people. I respect the dissatisfaction.|Every machine here makes me want to study harder and tighten a bolt.|People have always looked up and made stories. Here they also made a launch schedule.|I would like to know who designed the work clothes. Practical ambition has seams too.|The island is small enough to walk across and still points at something enormous.")
row('SOOTOPOLIS_CITY','soft_smile',"A city inside a crater makes scale feel like a personal oversight.|There are a lot of stairs between a delivery and a successful delivery.|Everything echoes here as though the city is quietly singing back.|I would open a bakery halfway up the stairs. A public service and a sound business plan.|A natural amphitheatre. I shall refrain from discovering how far my voice carries.|The city has a dramatic backstory written into the walls. Considerate of it.|The water, the stone, the access routes. Building here meant negotiating with every part of the place.|A place like this attracts very certain stories. I still want to hear the uncertain ones.|Good stairs should have places to rest. Even beautiful cities need knees.|The white walls turn the water's colour into light. I could stare for an embarrassing length of time.")
row('PACIFIDLOG_TOWN','soft_smile',"A floating town. The measurements keep moving while I try to write them down.|Flexible plans seem a useful local skill.|The town creaks in time with the sea. An orchestra with a complicated address.|A dropped spoon is a much larger event here than in most kitchens.|Every arrival here feels like stepping onto a stage that can change its mind.|The house has a tide. I shall stop complaining about creaky floorboards.|The supports move instead of fighting the water. That is an elegant compromise.|A town can be rooted in something other than the ground.|A secure pocket is not a luxury here. The sea operates a strict lost-property policy.|The reflections never quite agree with the houses. I like the conversation.")
row('EVER_GRANDE_CITY','soft_smile',"We have come a long way. I am trying to notice that before thinking about the next test.|Last stretch. Check the team, check the supplies, breathe.|It feels like standing backstage before a very large performance. You do not have to pretend otherwise.|I packed the good trail bread. This is the sort of day it was for.|A grand approach to a grand challenge. We can still take it one ordinary step at a time.|The flowers have no idea this is supposed to be intimidating. I appreciate their priorities.|A long journey is a lot of small things that held together. You helped them hold.|People remember the victory. I hope somebody remembers how hard the walking was.|Your team has carried you here. Make room for them in the celebration too.|I want a sketch before we go in. Not because the ending is certain. Because we reached this place.")
row('PALLET_TOWN','soft_smile',"OAK keeps his laboratory close to ordinary life. There is something sensible about that.|Small town, long roads out. I know the arrangement.|A quiet town gives the first note somewhere to start.|Somebody must be feeding all those lab assistants. A promising line of inquiry.|A place from which an excellent story might begin without knowing it yet.|A whole town that can hear someone call you home. A complicated comfort.|I like the practical lab. Less posing, more things being used.|Many large stories begin somewhere that still needs its windows cleaned.|I like seeing washing outside important people's houses. Very grounding.|The sea gives this little place more horizon than it knows what to do with.")
row('VIRIDIAN_CITY','neutral',"That old lesson on catching Pokemon matters more than people probably tell the teacher.|A town at the meeting of several roads. Somebody here has heard every excuse for being late.|It sounds busier than PALLET without quite becoming a crowd.|We should learn where the shop is before claiming we are fully prepared.|The gym is keeping a very dramatic schedule. I distrust a venue that enjoys mystery this much.|A closed door is not necessarily an invitation to invent a conspiracy. Tempting, though.|There is more to a useful town than its largest building being open.|Travellers bring a road into a town every time they ask what comes next.|A good travel outfit starts with supplies, not a heroic pose outside the gym.|Look at the way the paths lead the eye out of town. It is hard to stand still here.")
row('PEWTER_CITY','neutral',"A fossil has more history than the little label can reasonably fit. I would like a longer afternoon.|A museum is a good place to remember that a thing can matter without being useful to us.|The museum has the sort of quiet that makes a cough feel like a public performance.|I am resisting the temptation to describe every fossil as an extremely old lunch.|The museum has excellent dramatic material. I shall endeavour not to improve the dates.|Ancient remains and tidy labels. Time has become remarkably well organised.|Someone made those delicate displays hold together. I hope they are proud of the quiet work.|There are things we know from a fossil and things we would like to imagine. Both deserve honest labels.|The old stone and the careful displays work well together. Nobody needed to cover everything in ribbon.|I want to draw the fossils without making them look like trophies.")
row('CERULEAN_CITY','soft_smile',"The water makes a familiar street feel like a new set of reflections.|Watch the bike display. Admiring something does not qualify as a deposit.|The city seems to have a tune that changes when you cross a bridge.|A city of bridges needs a bakery on both sides. Logistical equality.|A bridge is a marvellous place for an entrance until somebody else needs to cross it.|The water looks very peaceful for a place with this much foot traffic.|Those bridges are the real shared project. Everyone depends on them without thinking about it.|People attach stories to bridges. Usually they are really stories about who waited on the other side.|The local bikes make me want to design a coat that will not fight the wheels.|The reflected city has slightly better hair. I am trying not to take it personally.")
row('VERMILION_CITY','neutral',"The harbour is full of experiments with very practical success conditions. Staying afloat, mainly.|Tie it properly. A knot is not convinced by optimism.|Somebody here knows a sea shanty with a second verse that makes sense.|A ship's kitchen is a heroic feat of balance. I would like to meet its flour supplier.|An international audience! I shall begin by learning how not to block the gangway.|A harbour is mostly people waiting for someone to come back. That part gets left out of postcards.|A busy ship is a machine with a community inside it. Every repair affects somebody.|The port is a good place to ask where a story has travelled before it reached you.|Good travelling clothes need to survive salt and being packed badly. I can help with one of those.|I could spend a week drawing the hands of people who work with rope.")
row('LAVENDER_TOWN','sad',"People remember their partners in different ways. I do not need to measure the difference.|We can take our time here.|The quiet has weight here. I would rather listen than fill it.|If someone wants company after visiting the tower, I can sit with them.|This is a place for being sincere without a flourish.|It is all right if we do not know what to say.|Some kinds of care are staying after there is nothing you can fix.|A story about a ghost often begins with somebody being missed.|A ribbon or a flower can matter because somebody chose it carefully.|The town has ordinary daily life beside its grief. Both belong in the picture.")
row('CELADON_CITY','laughing',"A very large store can still fail to sell the thing I came in for: restraint.|There is a great deal of shopping between us and the exit. Stay focused.|The city has enough competing noises to keep a nervous musician comfortably anonymous.|A department store should include a place to sit with a pastry. I am willing to petition.|A city with this many distractions is either ideal for a magician or terrible for his savings.|The shopping has developed a skyline. I respect the escalation.|I would like to inspect the lift. Preferably before Nico attempts to use it to tour every food counter.|Big cities have small stories too. Ask the person watering a plant, not only the person making speeches.|I can look at beautiful clothes without buying them. I may need you to remind me twice.|A city can have a colour palette and still refuse to stay inside it. I admire that.")
row('FUCHSIA_CITY','soft_smile',"A nature guide begins by admitting you have not met everything yet.|Wild places are not storage rooms. Leave room for the residents to be themselves.|The city gives the wildlife a louder part in the soundtrack.|I packed food so we would not have to guess which local berry is a dramatic mistake.|An audience with fur, feathers, and no patience for a bad trick. Excellent standards.|A nature guide should probably include a chapter about confused visitors.|The gates and paths are useful only if they are good for the Pokemon as well as the tourists.|A creature can matter without being rare enough for somebody to write a legend.|Practical clothes for a long walk. The Pokemon are unlikely to grade the outfit.|I want to draw something ordinary here before I go looking for something spectacular.")
row('SAFFRON_CITY','neutral',"A city of offices and psychic talent. Somewhere here a meeting has ended before it began.|Do not let somebody who reads minds convince you that you should have read the directions.|All these tall buildings must be full of little private rehearsals.|I wonder whether a psychic can tell you need lunch before you admit it.|Mind reading is going to make my profession significantly more embarrassing.|A psychic city. At last, a place where overthinking has civic infrastructure.|SILPH makes impressive things. I am interested in who gets to decide how they are used.|Somebody knowing a thought does not mean they understand the whole person.|A busy city needs places people can stop without buying something.|I like finding one warm window among all those straight lines.")
row('CINNABAR_ISLAND','neutral',"The lab asks what can return. I hope it also asks what will help it live well afterwards.|The volcano makes a useful point about keeping an exit plan.|I have never wanted a rehearsal space less likely to shake.|Volcanic bread oven: an idea for somebody with substantially better insurance.|An island with a volcano does not need my help being dramatic.|The island has built next to a very large reminder that plans are suggestions.|A laboratory beside a volcano makes maintenance a philosophical commitment.|Bringing something back is not the same thing as knowing what kind of home it needs.|A good outfit for this island has breathable fabric and no confidence near lava.|The black stone makes every little plant look remarkably determined.")
row('INDIGO_PLATEAU','soft_smile',"Whatever happens inside, the work your team has done is already real.|We have checked what we can. You do not have to feel fearless to begin.|Take a breath before the first note. There is time.|A proper meal, a checked bag, a team ready to try. A good beginning for an ending.|The stage is large. We can still meet it as ourselves.|You are allowed to be nervous here. It does not cancel the journey.|Trust your preparation enough to notice when the plan needs changing.|A final chapter is still made of ordinary choices about how to treat people.|Your team does not need a perfect speech. They need you paying attention.|I will remember the doorway as well as whatever is on the other side.")
row('BROCK','soft_smile',"He knows the rocks and the Pokemon. A useful reminder not to confuse the two fields.|A steady trainer. The sort who checks whether a plan can carry its weight.|He does not need to be loud to hold the room. I would like to learn that.|Rock-solid is the obvious joke. I am heroically leaving it on the plate.|A man with that much stone does not need help making an entrance.|He has made being grounded into a professional qualification.|Simple structure, well understood. Difficult to improve by adding unnecessary sparks.|It is nice to meet someone who respects old things without asking them to explain the future.|Practical clothes, clear idea. There is a lot to like about a consistent design.|He notices details in rocks I would have drawn as one grey shape.")
row('MISTY','neutral',"She understands water as a whole environment, not just a move category.|She expects you to keep up. We can do that without pretending we are never tired.|She has a very clear tempo. Coming in late is going to be noticeable.|Water everywhere and not one sensible place to put a hot oven. A challenging venue.|An aquatic stage! I shall perform from a safe, dry, entirely unheroic distance.|She looks like she would consider an excuse an optional warm-up exercise.|The pool makes the challenge clear. A design you can actually understand before testing it.|Confidence and kindness are allowed to live in the same person.|Her outfit does exactly what she needs it to do. That is good design.|Water reflects every movement. This room refuses to let a battle be visually boring.")
row('LT_SURGE','neutral',"Experience matters. So does allowing a new observation to change an old conclusion.|He has been through things. We can respect that without agreeing fear is the best teacher.|That voice could reach the back of a hall without a microphone.|I suspect he considers a generous portion a tactical necessity.|An entrance loud enough to qualify as weather. I shall adjust my professional goals.|He has the energy of a man who has never apologised to a door for startling it.|I would like permission to inspect the wiring after the battle. From an appropriate distance.|People carry their past into a room in different ways. His arrives loudly.|Those boots are ready for actual work. I have questions about the rest of the safety plan.|He makes it hard to forget a battle is physical work, not just a diagram.")
row('ERIKA','soft_smile',"She has made room for patience in a competitive place. I admire that.|Calm is not the same thing as unprepared. Watch carefully.|She leaves silence between her words. It makes people listen.|The garden smells like it would improve a disappointing afternoon.|An entrance with no flourish at all. Disturbingly effective.|She has a soft voice and absolutely no need to explain why you should take it seriously.|Careful cultivation is engineering with a much longer conversation.|There are many ways to be strong. Quiet ones tend to need fewer speeches.|Her clothes are beautiful, but the way she moves comfortably in them is the important part.|The plants make the room feel designed with living things rather than around them.")
row('KOGA','neutral',"Poison research requires exact handling and a great deal less bravado than people expect.|A hidden obstacle is still an obstacle. We can slow down and look.|The room is very good at making you mistrust the next step.|I am applying my strict rule about unidentified ingredients to this entire gym.|A professional in misdirection. I would like to compare notes from outside the trap.|He has made suspicion into interior design. Thorough commitment.|Invisible barriers make an impressive test and a difficult maintenance report.|The stories about ninjas rarely include who repairs the floor afterwards.|Freedom of movement matters. So does knowing what not to brush against.|A room designed to fool the eye makes me want to draw exactly what is actually there.")
row('SABRINA','flustered',"I would prefer to ask what she knows rather than constructing an experiment without her consent.|Reading a thought does not give somebody the right to decide what you meant.|If she hears my rehearsal voice, I hope she knows it is not the finished version.|I am trying to think of something more dignified than lunch. It is making lunch louder.|I have a professional interest in this and a personal interest in keeping my thoughts inside.|I have spent years editing my words. Direct access to the drafts feels unfair.|A mind is not a machine I am entitled to open just because I am curious.|There is more to a person than the strangest thing they can do.|She deserves to choose how much of herself to explain, just like anyone else.|I wonder what it feels like when everyone looks at you and sees only the astonishing part.")
row('BLAINE','laughing',"The questions are part of the test. I am resisting the urge to append a bibliography.|A quiz and a battle. At least he tells you there will be a test.|He has the timing of someone who enjoys waiting for an answer.|A fire gym. I would like to discuss ovens after we discuss not burning the team.|A man who understands that suspense benefits from a question mark.|He has found a way to make homework physically warmer.|Heat, timing, clear instructions. We agree about several things and disagree about the room temperature.|Knowing an answer can be useful. Knowing when to ask a better question matters too.|A strong personal style. I would recommend fabric that does not resent the climate.|The light here changes everything. Even an ordinary shadow looks like it is making a point.")
row('GIOVANNI','annoyed',"He treats living partners as proof of a theory about himself. I object to the method.|He mistakes obedience for loyalty. Expensive misunderstanding.|There is a performance here, and other people are paying for the stage.|I do not like a man who makes everyone else feel they have to earn a place at the table.|A costume and a commanding voice do not turn cruelty into authority.|He keeps giving his selfishness a very large chair.|A system that only works for the person at the top is not a successful system.|He tells a story that leaves everyone he hurts outside the frame.|People are not accessories. Neither are their Pokemon.|The room wants us to look at him. I keep looking at who has to stand behind him.")
row('PROF_OAK','soft_smile',"He asks people to go and look. A notebook should be an invitation, not a wall.|He trusts people with a responsibility and a way to begin. That helps.|He sounds interested in the answer before you have finished saying it.|A busy laboratory needs a good lunch arrangement. I am choosing to call this research.|A famous man who still has to find his own notes. Wonderfully reassuring.|He has somehow made homework sound like permission to have an adventure.|Useful equipment and enough freedom to learn with it. I like the arrangement.|A good storyteller knows when to send someone out to collect their own story.|A lab coat that has actually been used is more interesting than a perfect one.|I like the maps people have pinned up because they needed them, not because they matched the wall.")
row('BLUE','neutral',"He has prepared carefully. Confidence does not make the preparation less real.|He wants to be ahead. You do not have to let him choose the shape of your journey.|He speaks like the applause has already begun. I find that exhausting and slightly impressive.|We can compete without turning every meeting into the last slice of cake.|A strong entrance. I hope he eventually learns a victory is not an entire personality.|He looks as though being second would require rewriting his birth certificate.|His team has a plan. Notice that before reacting to the speech.|A rival can be part of your story without becoming its narrator.|Confidence wears better when it leaves somebody else room to breathe.|He is trying very hard to control the picture. The interesting part is what happens when he cannot.")
row('LORELEI','neutral',"Her control makes the difficult part look quiet. I would like to know how long she practised.|Steady voice. Steady plan. We should be equally deliberate.|There is no hurry in her timing. That makes every pause feel intentional.|I have several questions about keeping refreshments warm in this room. Later.|A wonderfully controlled atmosphere. I have never felt so politely intimidated.|The room has excellent composure. My hands have not adopted the local custom.|Keeping a system that stable takes work people rarely get to see.|Cold does not mean unfeeling. It is a mistake stories make too often.|Nothing is getting in the way of her work. A very clear design.|The ice catches colour where I expected plain white. Look carefully before deciding a thing is simple.")
row('BRUNO','neutral',"He has built an understanding through practice. The notes would probably be shorter than the work.|Strength like that comes from showing up on ordinary days.|You can hear his breathing before he speaks. He is taking the work seriously.|I suspect he has a very clear opinion about a proper breakfast.|No misdirection at all. A profoundly inconvenient style for my profession.|He has muscles in places I have only used for anxiety.|A strong structure still needs recovery. I hope he teaches that part too.|Some kinds of knowledge live in your hands before you can explain them.|Clothes that leave room for actual movement. We agree on the essentials.|I want to draw the way he stands, not just the shape of him. There is a difference.")
row('AGATHA','neutral',"She has seen enough to dislike easy explanations. I would like to ask a careful question.|Do not confuse being older with being finished. She certainly does not.|She has the voice of someone who knows exactly how long a silence should last.|I would not try to improve her recipe without being invited. Any recipe.|A performer who can command a room without standing up. I am studying the technique.|I suspect she can identify an excuse before you have finished inventing it.|Old expertise deserves attention. It can also survive a respectful question.|I would like to hear which ghost stories she thinks get the ghosts wrong.|A walking stick can be practical and part of a remarkable silhouette.|She has no interest in making the picture comfortable. I respect that more than I find it reassuring.")
row('LANCE','surprised',"He knows powerful partners individually. That matters more than the category on the team sheet.|Big team, clear responsibility. Pay attention to how he carries it.|A cape can be a sort of held note. His has excellent sustain.|I would like to know how much food that team requires before offering to cater.|The cape is magnificent. I am professional enough to admit when somebody has won that contest.|He has brought a dramatic breeze indoors. I did not know that was a qualification.|Working safely around a team like that takes more than enthusiasm.|A dragon is still a living Pokemon before it becomes somebody's symbol.|A convincing cape needs to move well. His has passed inspection.|He looks like the picture people imagine before they know how much ordinary work sits behind it.")

# Reactions to the resolved paired Kanto scenes, never offered before resolution.
row('scene0','laughing',"Reclassifying the potato was the most productive scientific meeting I have attended this week.|The potato is now assigned to lunch. Sensible transfer of department.|The potato has left the rock band. Creative differences.|I support recognising a potato's culinary potential before it becomes geology.|A rock transformed into lunch! An alarming new competitor.|The potato has survived an identity crisis with its dignity largely intact.|Good correction. The important part is not building a machine around the wrong potato.|A story about a stone has become a story about lunch. A happier ending than most stones get.|A potato need not resemble BROCK to deserve a place in the collection. Different collection, admittedly.|I am going to draw the potato. It has endured enough unsolicited criticism.")
row('scene1','laughing',"They established a control group. Unfortunately it was another umbrella.|Testing complete. They have invented being less wet.|Their research has a pleasantly simple chorus: still an umbrella.|Now they need the revolutionary companion invention: a warm towel.|The great unveiling reveals an umbrella. Audiences do enjoy a familiar ending.|A waterproof umbrella. At last, an invention brave enough to meet its own description.|A product that does the job. I have seen more complicated things do less.|An ordinary useful thing is allowed to be a satisfying discovery.|Good. Now someone can concentrate on a coat with pockets that actually stay dry.|I would paint the triumph. The umbrella would occupy a very modest part of it.")
row('scene2','laughing',"Replacing an address with humming is an excellent privacy improvement.|A song no longer doubles as delivery instructions. Good revision.|Humming is a legitimate lyric choice. Especially when the alternative is a home address.|A good chorus should bring people together without delivering them uninvited to your kitchen.|They have improved the song and its security in one elegant rewrite.|I admire an edit that protects both the metre and the front door.|Removing an unnecessary feature improved the whole design. Happens more often than I admit.|A song belongs to its singer. Their address need not belong to the whole harbour.|A useful alteration. The second verse now has room to breathe.|I want to remember the moment they realised humming was allowed.")
row('scene3','soft_smile',"Remembering the colour was a small question that made the care more particular.|They asked what she liked. That is worth doing properly.|Yellow flowers. Sometimes a little detail carries the whole feeling.|We can bring water next time. Care works better when it is not only ceremonial.|No grand gesture needed. The right colour was enough.|Little suns. I think I will remember that description.|They made something they can keep tending. That is a good kind of promise.|The flowers will change. The act of coming back is part of remembering too.|Someone chose a colour because of a person. That is what gives it meaning.|I would leave space for the person who is missing if I drew this. The flowers are not the whole picture.")
row('scene4','laughing',"The savings notebook has acquired an unexpectedly large experimental cost.|The first lesson is paid for. Let us hope they do not purchase a second one.|A very expensive notebook to record not spending money. That is a difficult opening chord.|They could save on lunch by eating the receipt. I am not recommending it.|An impressive vanishing act. The money disappeared into a book about keeping it.|The notebook has become the antagonist of its own instruction manual.|Using both sides of the paper is a practical recovery. Start with the thing that is already there.|A small mistake is easier to learn from when your friend is not making you feel small.|The notebook is lovely. Perhaps it can also be the last purchase justified by being lovely.|I like that they can laugh and still change the plan.")
row('scene5','laughing',"A guide that includes the lost reader is finally describing its entire ecosystem.|A map back to the bench solves the problem better than a lecture.|They have added the audience to the cast. Excellent revision.|A nature guide ought to include a reliable route back to lunch.|The elusive guide-losing visitor has finally been documented in its natural habitat.|Human beings: locally abundant, frequently facing the wrong direction.|They noticed where the actual failure happened and changed the design. Good work.|A guide gets kinder when it remembers a reader can be tired or confused.|The map is a practical accessory. Far better than insisting the owner should simply be more careful.|I hope the illustration of a lost visitor looks treated with affection.")
row('scene6','laughing',"Predicting lunch would have been easier with a direct question. Research has options.|The future remains uncertain. The need for lunch is looking fairly reliable.|A prophecy with a lunch break. I could work with that schedule.|I foresaw lunch too. My talent is almost entirely meal-based.|An astonishing prediction! I am receiving a vision of somebody checking the time.|The future has been revealed, and it is asking what is in the bag.|If you need a result, check what people actually do. Also check whether they have eaten.|A prediction is a story about something that has not happened. It still needs permission to guide a person.|I predict comfortable shoes will continue to be useful. My powers are very practical.|I want to sketch the prophet looking at the lunch bag for supplementary evidence.")
row('scene7','laughing',"Geology has benefited from everyone agreeing to speak politely about the volcano.|No need to provoke a mountain just to sound informed.|I support an arrangement that keeps the volcano out of the argument.|I shall also keep my geological opinions below oven temperature.|A volcano has no need of my assistance in a dramatic dispute.|The mountain has won the argument by remaining a mountain.|Good policy. Observe the heat before volunteering a demonstration.|Some old warnings are just good manners around something bigger than you.|I am happy to follow the local dress code of not standing in lava.|The volcano can be the subject of a picture without becoming the subject of an argument.")
row('scene8','laughing',"Three pillows introduce a substantial weight penalty into the hiking plan.|Keep one pillow. Your knees would like representation on the planning committee.|A softer landing is nice. A lighter walk also has musical merit.|I would trade one pillow for lunch. Predictably, I would make that trade twice.|An expedition equipped for an exceptionally grand nap.|An ambitious attempt to take the bed for a walk.|The solution to discomfort cannot be adding every comfort to the pack.|Wanting to feel safe before a journey is understandable. We can help with the practical part.|Padding has a purpose. Three pillows have begun competing with the purpose.|I would draw the pack before they lighten it. As a respectful historical record.")
row('scene9','laughing',"A staring contest with a statue is a valuable demonstration of a flawed test condition.|The statue has an unfair advantage in scheduling.|An opponent with no blink and excellent stillness. I would not take that audition.|The statue can wait forever. People require snack intervals.|A performer so committed to character that it has become stone. Formidable.|The statue appears to have trained for exactly this event.|They have measured patience in something that has no eyelids. We may need a different tool.|There is a story in deciding when a contest was never fair to begin with.|I support a rematch against someone whose face has moving parts.|I want to capture the moment the human realises the statue is not nervous.")
row('scene10','laughing',"The birds did not recognise the bicycle bell as a bird. Reassuring peer review.|A bicycle bell is better at warning people than impressing birds. Keep the useful part.|A bell can imitate a bird poorly and still be an excellent bell.|I have imitated a kettle accidentally. The birds were equally unimpressed.|The audience has rejected the impersonation. A brutal but informative matinee.|The birds have declined to revise their entire language for the benefit of a bicycle.|A device that does one job well need not acquire a second, feathery career.|The birds get a vote in whether this counts as birdsong.|The bicycle does not have to become a bird to justify its accessories.|I admire the birds for offering an honest review by simply leaving.")
row('scene11','soft_smile',"A relaxation club that allows actual rest has avoided a common administrative failure.|No compulsory relaxing. That is how you know the rules were thought through.|I would like to join quietly and avoid being elected to provide the music.|A club with no urgent agenda has room for a very good sandwich.|I shall perform the remarkable feat of not volunteering to organise anything.|At last, a club where my lack of a speech is a valid contribution.|A useful system that knows when to stop running. I approve.|Sitting together can be the whole story for a while.|I hope the chairs are comfortable before anyone orders matching club outfits.|Nobody needs to make this moment productive before it is allowed to be pleasant.")
